X-Training-Artifact: Fictional phishing-awareness example; do not send
From: Employee Rewards <rewards@company.example>
To: employee@company.example
Reply-To: reward-claims@example.net
Subject: Employee reward coupon - action requested by 6 PM
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8

Hello Employee,

The message claims that you have been selected to receive an employee shopping coupon in recognition of your work.

It asks you to review the reward before 6 PM using the following placeholder:
[SIMULATION LINK REDACTED - NO LIVE URL]

It also references an attachment named:
Employee_Reward_Coupon.pdf.exe [filename only; no file is attached]

Regards,
Employee Rewards Team

--- Training analysis notes ---
- Reward lure: an unexpected benefit is used to attract attention.
- Urgency: the 6 PM deadline pressures the recipient to act quickly.
- Reply-To mismatch: the reply address uses an external example domain.
- Double extension: the filename ends in .exe, not .pdf.
- Safe response: do not click, reply, or open an unexpected attachment; report it using the organization's approved channel.
